<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Mid-run failover from the active access leg to the standby one, driven by
    INET's ScenarioManager (see [Config UplinkStandbyLeg] in
    omnetpp_uplink.ini).

    Which leg carries the traffic is tsnDeviceB's IPv4 next hop: 192.168.1.1 is
    ue[0], 192.168.1.2 is ue[1]. Moving the traffic therefore means editing a
    route, and ScenarioManager has no command for that
    (ScenarioManager.cc:99-114 is the complete list). <set-route> below is
    nascTime's own, provided by the RouteSwitcher module in the network: it
    edits the route through IIpv4RoutingTable, the same API a routing protocol
    would use, and the routing table drops its lookup cache on the change, so
    the next packet already takes the new leg.

    `destination` and `gateway` take module names here, the way the .ini names
    nodes; dotted-quad addresses work too. The "%eth0" matters: a UE is
    multi-homed, and a bare "ue[1]" resolves to its cellular address rather
    than the Ethernet port that faces the TSN island.

    Nothing else in the network is touched. Both UEs forward IP throughout, the
    two legs keep distinct MAC addresses, and the switch keeps a static
    forwarding entry per leg (fdb_both.txt), so the traffic sits on exactly one
    leg at any moment and never gets flooded to both.

    Note that this is a scripted change and not a reaction to failure. INET
    does no next-hop liveness checking, and on an interface state change
    Ipv4RoutingTable only rebuilds netmask routes (Ipv4RoutingTable.cc:173):
    a static route pointing at a dead next hop stays. Failover that discovers
    the break by itself needs a routing protocol; INET ships several under
    inet/routing (rip, ospfv2, bgpv4, ...).
-->
<scenario>
    <at t="2s">
        <set-route module="routeSwitcher" host="tsnDeviceB"
                   destination="tsnDeviceA" gateway="ue[1]%eth0"/>
    </at>
</scenario>
